clean_summary:
Big world leads to runtime learning. The workshop on RL in Big Worlds at @RL_Conference focuses on ideas that enable agents to achieve goals in environments vastly more complex than themselves.

This requires giving agents the ability to learn continually and use approximate value functions, models, and policies effectively.

@rlc_bigworlds: "RL in Big Worlds is a workshop at @RL_Conference about ideas that enable agents to achieve goals in environments vastly more complex than themselves."

It is good to have a well-funded place that understands that intelligence is not about distilling human knowledge and skills into neural networks. Most current AI systems, while amazing, outsource the discovery of knowledge to humans. Systems that learn from human knowledge will, in the limit, be able to know and do everything that humans can do currently. These systems are immensely valuable but will be continually superseded by humans who can use these systems and learn from their own experience.

Systems that learn from their own experience will, in the limit, be able to do things that no human can do now and that no human would even be able to do. The latter systems would be vastly more powerful.

